1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,408
Why is the sky blue?

2
00:00:01,728 --> 00:00:04,864
We asked two versions
of the same small language model.

3
00:00:05,184 --> 00:00:08,747
Both answers come from the course
staff's saved Colab run.

4
00:00:08,983 --> 00:00:11,159
The original answers like a textbook.

5
00:00:11,479 --> 00:00:16,130
The fine-tuned one opens with a cheesy
greeting in the instructor's voice,

6
00:00:16,130 --> 00:00:17,261
and then explains.

7
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:18,908
Now check the physics.

8
00:00:19,228 --> 00:00:22,385
One blames refraction,
the other water molecules.

9
00:00:22,705 --> 00:00:26,375
Both are wrong: air molecules
scatter blue light more than red.

10
00:00:26,695 --> 00:00:30,151
The voice changed completely;
the facts did not get better.

11
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:33,032
Chat models learn their habits the same way;

12
00:00:33,032 --> 00:00:36,800
the lecture shows one that starts
with the words, Sure, here's.

13
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:43,947
This one kept 596 million weights frozen
and trained 1.15 million new ones.

14
00:00:44,267 --> 00:00:47,646
It took about 3 and a half
minutes on an A100,

15
00:00:47,646 --> 00:00:50,731
and fits in a file of about 4.6 megabytes.

16
00:00:50,967 --> 00:00:53,036
So what did it learn, and what didn't it?

17
00:00:53,356 --> 00:00:54,786
Two tracks answer that.

18
00:00:55,106 --> 00:00:59,098
In track A,
the README, Gemini writes a persona dataset

19
00:00:59,098 --> 00:01:01,420
and Google tunes Gemini for you.

20
00:01:01,740 --> 00:01:03,582
In track B, the course Colab,

21
00:01:03,582 --> 00:01:07,265
you fine-tune an open model yourself
and keep the weights.

22
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:09,895
The tracker, top left, shows the step;

23
00:01:09,895 --> 00:01:13,047
the gray tag,
top right, shows the lecture slides.

24
00:01:13,367 --> 00:01:16,375
When a tag says your turn,
pause and run the command.

25
00:01:18,883 --> 00:01:23,963
One honest limit:
we recorded this on October 31, 2026,

26
00:01:23,963 --> 00:01:27,566
with no service account key and no GPU.

27
00:01:27,886 --> 00:01:30,783
Every README command ran exactly as written;

28
00:01:30,783 --> 00:01:34,273
the ones that talk
to Google stop at the missing key,

29
00:01:34,273 --> 00:01:36,248
and we keep those real errors.

30
00:01:36,568 --> 00:01:40,473
Working outputs come from the README
and the saved Colab run.

31
00:01:43,317 --> 00:01:45,962
There are three ways to change
what a model says.

32
00:01:46,282 --> 00:01:49,120
A prompt changes the input
every time you ask.

33
00:01:49,440 --> 00:01:54,197
Retrieval, the RAG app from lecture 5,
adds looked-up text to the input.

34
00:01:54,517 --> 00:01:56,778
Both leave the model itself untouched.

35
00:01:57,017 --> 00:02:00,807
Fine-tuning turns the third dial:
it changes the weights,

36
00:02:00,807 --> 00:02:03,910
by showing the model
many examples of an input

37
00:02:03,910 --> 00:02:05,529
and the output you want.

38
00:02:05,849 --> 00:02:09,510
In this course
that usually means instruction fine-tuning:

39
00:02:09,510 --> 00:02:11,972
pairs of an instruction and its answer.

40
00:02:12,292 --> 00:02:15,001
A persona is a task-specific fine-tune.

41
00:02:15,250 --> 00:02:18,194
Compare a model
that was never instruction-tuned.

42
00:02:18,514 --> 00:02:24,167
The notebook gives OPT 350m the words,
hello, my cheese name is.

43
00:02:24,487 --> 00:02:25,878
It simply keeps going:

44
00:02:25,878 --> 00:02:29,864
my cheese name is cheese,
I'm not sure if you're joking or not.

45
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:33,671
An instruction-tuned model
answers instead of continuing,

46
00:02:33,671 --> 00:02:36,052
often with a habit like, Sure, here's.

47
00:02:36,372 --> 00:02:39,785
Our fine-tuned Qwen answers
the way the instructor would.

48
00:02:40,105 --> 00:02:42,373
That's the lesson of the saved run:

49
00:02:42,373 --> 00:02:45,290
style is learnable;
facts are not guaranteed.

50
00:02:45,534 --> 00:02:50,230
A useful mental model:
fine-tuning moves the model's default voice;

51
00:02:50,230 --> 00:02:52,894
RAG changes the open book on its desk.

52
00:02:53,214 --> 00:02:56,990
In track A, Google trains the model
and you never see a weight.

53
00:02:57,310 --> 00:03:01,157
In track B,
you train a small adapter on a Colab GPU,

54
00:03:01,157 --> 00:03:02,899
and it is yours to save.

55
00:03:05,033 --> 00:03:10,537
Start in your workspace, the AC215 folder,
with Docker installed and running.

56
00:03:10,857 --> 00:03:15,486
The README only links the repository,
so cloning it is an extra step.

57
00:03:15,806 --> 00:03:18,345
Your prompt shows
your own user name and machine.

58
00:03:18,583 --> 00:03:21,676
Recorded at the commit on screen;
yours may be newer.

59
00:03:21,996 --> 00:03:26,421
Inside are two folders,
dataset-creator and gemini-finetuner,

60
00:03:26,421 --> 00:03:28,524
each with the same six files.

61
00:03:28,844 --> 00:03:31,837
The autotrain folder
is not part of the README,

62
00:03:31,837 --> 00:03:32,983
so leave it alone.

63
00:03:33,233 --> 00:03:35,580
Step 2 happens in the cloud console.

64
00:03:35,900 --> 00:03:40,799
Create a service account called
llm-service-account with two roles:

65
00:03:40,799 --> 00:03:45,478
Storage Admin, and Agent Platform User,
formerly Vertex AI User.

66
00:03:45,798 --> 00:03:49,852
Under keys, add a JSON key,
which downloads to your laptop.

67
00:03:50,172 --> 00:03:53,308
If you kept the account from lecture 5,
reuse it.

68
00:03:53,550 --> 00:03:58,670
Step 3: make a folder called secrets next
to the repository, not inside it.

69
00:03:58,990 --> 00:04:04,629
Move the key in and name it exactly
llm-service-account.json;

70
00:04:04,629 --> 00:04:09,806
with hidden file extensions
it can end up as .json.json.

71
00:04:10,126 --> 00:04:12,003
Ours stays empty on purpose.

72
00:04:12,323 --> 00:04:13,667
Watch what that does.

73
00:04:13,916 --> 00:04:15,751
Why next to the repository?

74
00:04:16,071 --> 00:04:19,655
A key outside the clone
can never be committed by accident.

75
00:04:19,975 --> 00:04:24,417
The env.dev file looks two folders up
from wherever the script runs,

76
00:04:24,417 --> 00:04:27,356
so both container folders
reach the same key.

77
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:31,836
An extra step 4,
implied by the file's own comments:

78
00:04:31,836 --> 00:04:37,456
in env.dev, set your own project ID
and bucket name on lines 6 and 7.

79
00:04:37,776 --> 00:04:40,186
The scripts pass both into the containers.

80
00:04:40,433 --> 00:04:45,389
The README also assumes the Agent Platform
and Storage APIs are enabled,

81
00:04:45,389 --> 00:04:47,110
and a bucket of your own.

82
00:04:47,430 --> 00:04:51,526
Bucket names are global,
and cheese-dataset belongs to the course.

83
00:04:51,846 --> 00:04:54,001
Pause here and finish all four steps.

84
00:04:57,150 --> 00:05:01,886
Step 1 of the dataset section:
go into the dataset-creator folder.

85
00:05:02,206 --> 00:05:04,937
Step 2: sh docker-shell.sh.

86
00:05:05,257 --> 00:05:07,326
The first build installs everything.

87
00:05:07,646 --> 00:05:10,686
Here it took 45 seconds
on a fast connection;

88
00:05:10,686 --> 00:05:12,105
expect a few minutes.

89
00:05:12,425 --> 00:05:15,497
If you see source:
not found, the fix is coming.

90
00:05:15,733 --> 00:05:17,077
What did the script do?

91
00:05:17,397 --> 00:05:22,803
It reads env.dev, builds the image,
and runs it with two folders mounted:

92
00:05:22,803 --> 00:05:27,765
this folder becomes /app,
and your secrets folder becomes /secrets.

93
00:05:28,085 --> 00:05:32,032
Three variables carry the key's path,
your project and your bucket.

94
00:05:32,352 --> 00:05:36,491
The --rm flag removes the container
when you exit.

95
00:05:36,733 --> 00:05:38,281
Read the banner kindly:

96
00:05:38,281 --> 00:05:41,647
it says activated
before the activation line runs,

97
00:05:41,647 --> 00:05:44,370
and the prompt never shows an app prefix.

98
00:05:44,690 --> 00:05:45,964
Both are harmless;

99
00:05:45,964 --> 00:05:49,267
an extra check shows
Python comes from /.venv,

100
00:05:49,267 --> 00:05:51,133
and your settings arrived.

101
00:05:51,453 --> 00:05:55,118
The part after app
and the at sign is the container ID;

102
00:05:55,118 --> 00:05:56,317
yours will differ.

103
00:05:56,567 --> 00:06:02,391
Step 3 is the README's tip:
ls -la /secrets, inside the container.

104
00:06:02,711 --> 00:06:06,892
With a key,
you'd see llm-service-account.json here.

105
00:06:07,212 --> 00:06:10,199
Ours shows only the folder itself,
on purpose.

106
00:06:10,433 --> 00:06:13,953
Step 4: python cli.py --help.

107
00:06:14,273 --> 00:06:17,238
Three flags: generate, prepare and upload.

108
00:06:17,558 --> 00:06:18,842
It works without a key,

109
00:06:18,842 --> 00:06:22,806
because the code only looks for credentials
when it first calls Google.

110
00:06:23,126 --> 00:06:24,876
Pause, and get to this prompt.

111
00:06:27,117 --> 00:06:30,189
Two traps,
shown as extras from a second terminal.

112
00:06:30,509 --> 00:06:36,624
On Debian, Ubuntu or WSL, sh is dash,
which has no source command:

113
00:06:36,624 --> 00:06:39,682
run bash docker-shell.sh instead.

114
00:06:40,002 --> 00:06:44,617
And from the repository root,
the script cannot find env.dev:

115
00:06:44,617 --> 00:06:46,509
cd into the folder first.

116
00:06:47,933 --> 00:06:52,776
One more extra:
docker images reports 1.64 gigabytes.

117
00:06:53,096 --> 00:06:55,878
The lockfile installs 133 packages,

118
00:06:55,878 --> 00:07:00,012
among them the RAG
lecture's chromadb and langchain,

119
00:07:00,012 --> 00:07:02,397
which this code never imports.

120
00:07:06,633 --> 00:07:10,833
The lecture's instruction dataset
has three parts per example:

121
00:07:10,833 --> 00:07:14,761
an instruction, optional context,
and the output you want.

122
00:07:15,081 --> 00:07:20,052
The tutorial drops the context:
each example is a question and an answer.

123
00:07:20,372 --> 00:07:21,972
Who writes a hundred of them?

124
00:07:22,292 --> 00:07:23,998
Another model, a teacher.

125
00:07:24,233 --> 00:07:29,545
Here the teacher is Gemini 3.1 Flash Lite,
called in the global location.

126
00:07:29,865 --> 00:07:33,538
It gets a system instruction
describing the persona,

127
00:07:33,538 --> 00:07:36,081
an input prompt asking for 20 pairs,

128
00:07:36,081 --> 00:07:39,472
and a JSON schema
that fixes the answer's shape:

129
00:07:39,472 --> 00:07:41,449
question and answer objects.

130
00:07:42,583 --> 00:07:46,466
Step 1: python cli.py --generate.

131
00:07:46,786 --> 00:07:48,866
With a key, it costs cents:

132
00:07:48,866 --> 00:07:52,332
the README estimates
$2 per 1,000 iterations.

133
00:07:52,652 --> 00:07:55,462
Without one,
every batch prints the same line:

134
00:07:55,462 --> 00:07:57,111
the key file was not found.

135
00:07:57,431 --> 00:08:00,716
And yet the exit status is zero,
which means success.

136
00:08:01,036 --> 00:08:02,316
Remember that zero.

137
00:08:02,566 --> 00:08:07,323
Step 2 explains the system prompt,
which lives in cli.py.

138
00:08:07,643 --> 00:08:12,796
It tells the teacher to open each answer
with one of the instructor's catchphrases,

139
00:08:12,796 --> 00:08:15,217
such as good question, or glad you ask.

140
00:08:15,537 --> 00:08:20,166
And it asks for enthusiastic exclamations,
in the instructor's character.

141
00:08:20,416 --> 00:08:24,512
Step 3 is the input prompt,
asking for 20 diverse pairs.

142
00:08:24,832 --> 00:08:30,421
Step 4: the number of iterations
is a constant on line 170, not a flag;

143
00:08:30,421 --> 00:08:32,704
edit it for a bigger dataset.

144
00:08:33,024 --> 00:08:35,351
Temperature 0.9 adds variety,

145
00:08:35,351 --> 00:08:40,406
and the response schema makes
Gemini return JSON in that shape.

146
00:08:40,650 --> 00:08:44,877
With a key, the files would hold
answers like the README's samples:

147
00:08:44,877 --> 00:08:48,031
Yello, this is the instructor,
your cheese monker.

148
00:08:48,351 --> 00:08:54,547
A small slip: the README's prompt
excerpt lists older openers than cli.py,

149
00:08:54,547 --> 00:08:59,487
but cli.py's own samples still open
with Yello and Welcome.

150
00:08:59,807 --> 00:09:00,980
Expect a mix.

151
00:09:01,216 --> 00:09:03,371
One question before we move on.

152
00:09:03,691 --> 00:09:08,384
The prompt asks the teacher for specifics,
such as optimal pH levels.

153
00:09:17,233 --> 00:09:18,982
It becomes a training target.

154
00:09:19,302 --> 00:09:23,420
The student learns to say it,
confidently, in the instructor's voice.

155
00:09:23,740 --> 00:09:26,483
Synthetic data carries
the teacher's mistakes,

156
00:09:26,483 --> 00:09:28,390
so check what the teacher wrote.

157
00:09:29,733 --> 00:09:31,759
Now step 1 of the prepare section,

158
00:09:31,759 --> 00:09:34,618
as a student would type it right
after that run.

159
00:09:34,938 --> 00:09:38,117
Type python cli.py --prepare.

160
00:09:38,437 --> 00:09:42,106
Zero errors, a shape of zero by zero,
and then a crash.

161
00:09:42,426 --> 00:09:47,248
KeyError:
'question', from line 243 of cli.py.

162
00:09:47,483 --> 00:09:48,592
Trace it back.

163
00:09:48,912 --> 00:09:52,364
In generate,
each batch runs inside a try block,

164
00:09:52,364 --> 00:09:55,935
and the except
on line 200 catches any exception,

165
00:09:55,935 --> 00:09:57,830
prints it, and carries on.

166
00:09:58,150 --> 00:10:02,032
So no file was written,
and the command still exited with zero.

167
00:10:02,266 --> 00:10:05,137
Prepare found no files,
built an empty table,

168
00:10:05,137 --> 00:10:08,581
and failed the first time it read
the question column.

169
00:10:08,901 --> 00:10:10,686
The error was there all along;

170
00:10:10,686 --> 00:10:13,423
the catch-all only moved
it one command later.

171
00:10:13,743 --> 00:10:16,005
Read the lines, not the exit code.

172
00:10:16,250 --> 00:10:18,299
The fix is the key itself:

173
00:10:18,299 --> 00:10:22,160
put it in the secrets folder
with the exact name,

174
00:10:22,160 --> 00:10:24,570
and check with ls -la /secrets.

175
00:10:24,816 --> 00:10:29,256
So we can watch a real run, an extra:
five hand-written files,

176
00:10:29,256 --> 00:10:33,051
tagged scripted,
in exactly the shape Gemini returns.

177
00:10:33,371 --> 00:10:37,347
They hold 20 pairs,
where Gemini would write about a hundred;

178
00:10:37,347 --> 00:10:39,237
your generate writes its own.

179
00:10:39,557 --> 00:10:43,078
Note the 1-byte CSV
the failed run left behind.

180
00:10:43,316 --> 00:10:44,596
Now prepare again.

181
00:10:44,916 --> 00:10:49,417
Five files processed, zero errors,
and a shape of 19 by 2.

182
00:10:49,737 --> 00:10:53,535
The last scripted pair repeats
the first question on purpose.

183
00:10:53,855 --> 00:10:57,503
drop_duplicates on line 233 removed it.

184
00:10:57,823 --> 00:11:00,042
Every answer opens with a catchphrase.

185
00:11:00,283 --> 00:11:06,384
The rows are saved as instruct-dataset.csv:
the lecture's term, in the file name.

186
00:11:06,704 --> 00:11:09,499
Pause, and run
generate and prepare yourself.

187
00:11:12,833 --> 00:11:15,137
Step 2 of prepare is about format.

188
00:11:15,457 --> 00:11:16,694
Follow one pair.

189
00:11:17,014 --> 00:11:18,913
It starts as a CSV row.

190
00:11:19,233 --> 00:11:25,215
Lines 241 to 247 glue it into one text
with human and bot labels,

191
00:11:25,215 --> 00:11:30,369
the text column the repository's autotrain
folder reads.

192
00:11:30,689 --> 00:11:32,374
Track A doesn't use that one.

193
00:11:32,616 --> 00:11:37,091
Lines 251 to 257 build
what Gemini tuning expects:

194
00:11:37,091 --> 00:11:39,150
a list called contents,

195
00:11:39,150 --> 00:11:44,712
with a user turn and a model turn,
each holding parts and text.

196
00:11:44,950 --> 00:11:47,852
An extra look at the real train.jsonl:

197
00:11:47,852 --> 00:11:53,654
the same nesting as the README's example,
but each example on a single line.

198
00:11:53,974 --> 00:11:57,131
That's the L in JSONL: JSON lines.

199
00:11:57,366 --> 00:12:02,749
Then train_test_split
holds out 10 percent with a fixed seed:

200
00:12:02,749 --> 00:12:10,337
17 examples in train.jsonl, 2 in test.jsonl,
which the tuning job uses for validation.

201
00:12:10,657 --> 00:12:13,037
The code's comment says Gemini

202
00:12:13,037 --> 00:12:17,398
accepts at most 256 validation examples,
hence the cap.

203
00:12:21,100 --> 00:12:24,942
Whatever the wrapping,
training is still next-word prediction,

204
00:12:24,942 --> 00:12:26,924
now on the instructor's answers.

205
00:12:28,600 --> 00:12:30,411
An extra from the laptop side:

206
00:12:30,411 --> 00:12:33,187
the bind mount left these files
in your clone.

207
00:12:33,507 --> 00:12:38,178
git status shows the data folder as ignored,
so it stays out of your commits.

208
00:12:38,416 --> 00:12:43,237
Next section, upload:
python cli.py --upload.

209
00:12:43,557 --> 00:12:47,389
This time it fails loudly,
with a DefaultCredentialsError:

210
00:12:47,389 --> 00:12:48,389
file not found.

211
00:12:48,699 --> 00:12:51,216
With a key, no output is documented.

212
00:12:51,536 --> 00:12:54,939
Look for one line per file
starting with Uploading file,

213
00:12:54,939 --> 00:12:57,552
five in all, then find them in your bucket.

214
00:12:57,800 --> 00:13:01,569
A small slip:
the README never says to create that bucket,

215
00:13:01,569 --> 00:13:03,453
and upload assumes it exists.

216
00:13:03,773 --> 00:13:04,925
Make it first.

217
00:13:05,245 --> 00:13:08,723
If you just changed env.dev,
restart the container.

218
00:13:09,043 --> 00:13:10,536
Then pause and upload.

219
00:13:13,883 --> 00:13:15,611
On to the fine-tune section.

220
00:13:15,931 --> 00:13:18,843
Type exit; the container removes itself,

221
00:13:18,843 --> 00:13:22,118
and an extra docker ps
-a shows nothing left.

222
00:13:22,438 --> 00:13:25,488
Step 1: go to the gemini-finetuner folder.

223
00:13:26,133 --> 00:13:28,551
An extra edit the README leaves out:

224
00:13:28,551 --> 00:13:32,917
lines 10 and 11 still point
at the course bucket, cheese-dataset.

225
00:13:33,237 --> 00:13:36,778
Replace only cheese-dataset
with your bucket name;

226
00:13:36,778 --> 00:13:41,877
keep the folder, llm-finetune-dataset-small,
where upload put the files.

227
00:13:42,197 --> 00:13:46,357
The folder is mounted into the container,
so there's no need to rebuild.

228
00:13:46,600 --> 00:13:49,779
Step 2: sh docker-shell.sh again.

229
00:13:50,099 --> 00:13:53,618
It's instant:
every layer up to uv sync is cached,

230
00:13:53,618 --> 00:13:56,925
because both folders
share the same Dockerfile,

231
00:13:56,925 --> 00:14:00,232
pyproject and lockfile,
as an extra diff shows.

232
00:14:01,466 --> 00:14:07,162
Step 3: python cli.py --help
shows two flags, train and chat.

233
00:14:07,482 --> 00:14:11,343
The train client creates
tuning jobs in us-central1.

234
00:14:11,663 --> 00:14:16,513
The chat client calls the tuned model
in the multi-region location us,

235
00:14:16,513 --> 00:14:17,829
at its own address.

236
00:14:18,066 --> 00:14:22,186
The train function wraps your bucket files
as tuning datasets,

237
00:14:22,186 --> 00:14:25,575
and asks for one epoch
and an adapter of size four.

238
00:14:25,895 --> 00:14:26,844
By our reading,

239
00:14:26,844 --> 00:14:31,079
that's Google's managed version
of the idea you'll build in part 2.

240
00:14:31,399 --> 00:14:33,021
One call starts the job.

241
00:14:33,266 --> 00:14:39,026
Step 1 of the fine-tune model section:
python cli.py --train.

242
00:14:39,346 --> 00:14:41,650
The experimental warning is harmless.

243
00:14:41,970 --> 00:14:44,765
Then the missing key: no job was created.

244
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:49,902
With a key, look for Training job started,
a job name in us-central1,

245
00:14:49,902 --> 00:14:52,957
and after a minute, Tuned model info: None.

246
00:14:53,277 --> 00:14:56,154
Main calls train with wait_for_job=False,

247
00:14:56,154 --> 00:15:01,555
so the command hands in the job and leaves,
like a ticket at the dry cleaner.

248
00:15:01,875 --> 00:15:06,013
Before you pause, watch the cost table,
coming up in under a minute;

249
00:15:06,013 --> 00:15:08,082
then come back and start your job.

250
00:15:10,316 --> 00:15:13,915
Training needs far more
GPU memory than a laptop has;

251
00:15:13,915 --> 00:15:15,884
here that's Google's problem.

252
00:15:16,204 --> 00:15:20,230
Step 2: watch the job in the console
until it says succeeded,

253
00:15:20,230 --> 00:15:22,540
with a tuned model and an endpoint.

254
00:15:22,783 --> 00:15:24,340
A question to pause on.

255
00:15:33,183 --> 00:15:37,620
On Google's hardware in us-central1,
reading your bucket directly.

256
00:15:37,940 --> 00:15:41,994
Deleting the container loses nothing:
it only submitted the job.

257
00:15:43,933 --> 00:15:46,856
Step 1 of the cost section:
the README's table.

258
00:15:47,176 --> 00:15:50,922
At $0.008 per thousand tokens,

259
00:15:50,922 --> 00:15:56,791
16,444 documents over 3 epochs
come to $112.73;

260
00:15:56,791 --> 00:16:01,661
the table calls that way too much data.

261
00:16:01,981 --> 00:16:05,053
88 documents cost about 57 cents.

262
00:16:05,300 --> 00:16:10,659
A small slip: the table prices
Gemini 1.5 Flash for 3 epochs,

263
00:16:10,659 --> 00:16:14,964
while the code tunes
Gemini 3.1 Flash Lite for 1.

264
00:16:15,284 --> 00:16:19,614
Treat these numbers as stale
and check the pricing page the README links.

265
00:16:19,850 --> 00:16:22,282
Before you chat, one more extra edit.

266
00:16:22,602 --> 00:16:26,421
Line 107 still holds
the instructor's endpoint.

267
00:16:26,741 --> 00:16:29,023
Copy yours from the console:

268
00:16:29,023 --> 00:16:33,098
a path ending
in locations/us/endpoints and an ID.

269
00:16:33,418 --> 00:16:37,250
Paste it there; otherwise chat points
at the instructor's endpoint,

270
00:16:37,250 --> 00:16:38,794
which your key can't reach.

271
00:16:39,033 --> 00:16:42,596
Step 2: python cli.py --chat.

272
00:16:42,916 --> 00:16:46,117
It prints its question,
how is cheese made in Greece,

273
00:16:46,117 --> 00:16:47,929
answered the instructor's way.

274
00:16:48,249 --> 00:16:52,494
Then it prints the serving location,
us, and the same missing key.

275
00:16:52,814 --> 00:16:55,332
Pause and run it once your endpoint is in.

276
00:16:57,566 --> 00:17:01,871
The README shows a working
endpoint's answer to an older question:

277
00:17:01,871 --> 00:17:03,305
the same Yello opener.

278
00:17:03,625 --> 00:17:05,076
Your answer will differ.

279
00:17:05,316 --> 00:17:07,919
Step 3, the hosted demo, is optional.

280
00:17:08,239 --> 00:17:12,666
Step 4: the console's training monitor
shows your job's loss curves

281
00:17:12,666 --> 00:17:14,383
and its data distribution.

282
00:17:14,633 --> 00:17:17,118
That's track A: a teacher wrote the data,

283
00:17:17,118 --> 00:17:20,329
Google ran the job,
and you have an endpoint to call.

284
00:17:20,649 --> 00:17:22,548
And you never saw a single weight.

285
00:17:22,868 --> 00:17:25,236
In part 2, the weights are in your hands.

